/**
  @page guides-shape-optimization Shape and topology optimization
  @brief Shape derivatives and Hilbertian regularization in a level-set optimization loop.

  @tableofcontents

  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-shapeopt-intro Introduction

  Shape optimization seeks a domain @f$ \Omega @f$ minimizing an objective

  @f[
    J(\Omega) = \int_\Omega j(u_\Omega)\, dx + \ell\, |\Omega|,
  @f]

  where @f$ u_\Omega @f$ solves a PDE posed on @f$ \Omega @f$ (the *state
  equation*) and @f$ \ell @f$ is a Lagrange multiplier penalizing volume.
  The canonical benchmark is *compliance minimization*, where the state is linear
  elasticity and @f$ J @f$ is the work of the loads,
  @f$ \int_\Omega Ae(u):e(u) @f$. Rodin was designed around this problem
  class — this guide explains the moving parts and how the examples
  assemble them.

  Working examples, in increasing sophistication:

  | Example | What it shows |
  |---------|---------------|
  | @ref examples-shapeoptimization-simple_cantilever "SimpleCantilever2D.cpp" | Minimal compliance loop |
  | @ref examples-shapeoptimization-levelset_cantilever "LevelSetCantilever2D.cpp" | Level-set representation with remeshing |
  | [LevelSetCantilever3D.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/ShapeOptimization/LevelSetCantilever3D.cpp) | The same in 3D |
  | [LevelSetArch2D.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/ShapeOptimization/LevelSetArch2D.cpp), [LevelSetMast2D.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/ShapeOptimization/LevelSetMast2D.cpp) | Other load cases |
  | [LevelSetEigenvalue2D.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/ShapeOptimization/LevelSetEigenvalue2D.cpp) | Eigenvalue objective |
  | [BoundaryOptimization](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/tree/develop/examples/BoundaryOptimization) | Boundary-condition/support optimization |
  | @ref examples-density_optimization-index "DensityOptimization" | Density (SIMP-type) approaches |

  @section guides-shapeopt-derivative The shape derivative

  Perturbing the domain along a vector field @f$ \theta @f$,
  @f$ \Omega_t = (\mathrm{Id} + t\theta)(\Omega) @f$, the *shape
  derivative* is

  @f[
    J'(\Omega)(\theta) = \lim_{t \to 0} \frac{J(\Omega_t) - J(\Omega)}{t}.
  @f]

  The Hadamard structure theorem states that, for smooth data, the
  derivative concentrates on the boundary and depends only on the normal
  component.

  @f[
    J'(\Omega)(\theta) = \int_{\partial\Omega} g\; \theta \cdot n\; ds,
  @f]

  with a scalar density @f$ g @f$ computed from the state (and, for
  non-self-adjoint objectives, an adjoint state). For compliance with a
  traction-free optimizable boundary with fixed loads and supports,
  @f$g=\ell-Ae(u):e(u)@f$. A descent load therefore contains
  @f$Ae(u):e(u)-\ell@f$.

  Boundary and distributed forms represent the continuous differential.
  Their discrete realizations can differ:

  - **Boundary (Hadamard) form.** Assemble
    @f$ \int_\Gamma g\, \theta\cdot n @f$ on the interface facets. Exact in
    the continuous setting, but discretely *concentrated* on the interface.
    The density is quadratic in the strain and inherits mesh noise.
  - **Distributed (volume) form.** Assemble
    @f$ \int_\Omega E : \nabla\theta @f$ with an Eshelby-type tensor
    @f$ E @f$; integrating by parts with the state equation recovers the
    boundary form analytically. Discretely this yields a *smoother, more
    robust* gradient and is generally preferred.

  @note When implementing or modifying a shape-derivative density, verify
  it with a directional finite-difference test. Choose a localized
  @f$ \theta @f$, and compare the assembled @f$ \int E:\nabla\theta @f$
  against a central finite difference of the *discrete* objective under
  the mesh perturbation @f$ x \mapsto x \pm t\,\theta(x) @f$. An incorrect
  density leaves a residual that does not vanish at optimality; the
  symptom is a descent direction that keeps flagging regions the boundary
  has already reached.

  @section guides-shapeopt-hilbertian Hilbertian regularization from derivative to velocity

  The linear functional @f$ J'(\Omega) @f$ is represented by a vector field through
  a Riesz problem in an @f$ H^1 @f$-type inner product — this simultaneously *extends* the boundary information into
  the bulk and *smooths* it. This is the
  @ref Rodin::Hilbert::H1a "Hilbert::H1a" operator, which solves

  @f[
    \alpha^2 \int_D \nabla\theta : \nabla\xi + \int_D \theta\cdot\xi
      = -J'(\Omega)(\xi) \qquad \forall \xi,
  @f]

  and returns @f$ \theta @f$.

  @code{.cpp}
  using Space = decltype(vectorFes);
  using Field = GridFunction<Space, Math::Vector<Real>>;
  Hilbert::H1a<Field, Space> extension(vectorFes);
  extension.setAlpha(alpha);
  const auto& test = extension.getTestFunction();
  auto descentLoad = BoundaryIntegral(
    energy - ell, Dot(BoundaryNormal(mesh), test)).over(freeBoundary);
  auto theta = extension(descentLoad);
  @endcode

  Here `energy` is the elastic strain-energy contraction
  @f$Ae(u):e(u)@f$ and `freeBoundary` labels the optimizable boundary.
  The extension operator solves for the supplied load; the descent sign is
  supplied when that load is constructed.


  The parameter @f$ \alpha @f$ has units of length and is the
  *smoothing radius* of the descent direction:

  - larger @f$ \alpha @f$ → smoother, more global velocities; fewer
    mesh-frequency oscillations; slower resolution of fine features;
  - too small @f$ \alpha @f$ → the velocity inherits interface noise and
    the descent stagnates or oscillates.

  @section guides-shapeopt-levelset Level-set representation

  Representing the shape implicitly,
  @f$ \Omega = \{ x : \phi(x) < 0 \} @f$ with interface
  @f$ \Gamma = \{\phi = 0\} @f$, allows topology changes (holes merging
  and splitting) without any surgery. The shape is advanced by
  transporting @f$ \phi @f$ with the descent velocity — the
  Hamilton–Jacobi/advection step
  (@ref Rodin::Advection "Advection module").

  @f[
    \partial_t \phi + \theta \cdot \nabla\phi = 0.
  @f]

  The numerical transport procedure also determines the distance-field
  maintenance and step-size controls:

  1. **Redistancing.** Transport degrades the signed-distance property
     (@f$ |\nabla\phi| = 1 @f$); normals, band widths, and step-size
     control all silently assume it. Restore it periodically with the
     fast marching method (@ref Rodin::Eikonal "Eikonal::FMM") or the
     PDE-based models in @ref Rodin::Distance "Distance" (Eikonal,
     Poisson, SignedPoisson, or the Rvachev / SpaldingTucker
     normalizations).
  2. **Step-size (CFL) control.** The interface should move at most a
     bounded number of cells per iteration; combine a time-step cap with
     a line search on the true objective. A descent step accepted without
     an objective decrease check can jump out of the basin entirely (a
     failure mode in which material floods the whole bounding box).

  @section guides-shapeopt-discretization Getting a computable domain from the level set

  The state equation needs a domain to integrate over. The following
  representations supply it:

  - **Body-fitted discretization.** Cut the zero set into the mesh so
    that @f$ \Gamma @f$ becomes a set of interface facets.
    MMG's `LevelSetDiscretizer` produces the
    body-fitted mesh, `MMG::Adapt` and `MMG::Optimizer` maintain
    metric conformity and element quality across iterations. Accurate
    domain and interface integrals; the cost is remeshing every
    iteration.
  - **Interface registration (SWIFT).** Displace the existing mesh so its interface tracks @f$ \{\phi=0\} @f$ using the
    @ref Rodin::Adaptation "Adaptation" module's SWIFT solver. Keeps the
    mesh topology and connectivity stable between iterations.
  - **Ersatz material / density.** Keep a fixed mesh and interpolate
    material properties (void as weak material) — the
    @ref examples-density_optimization-index "DensityOptimization" family. No
    remeshing, at the price of a modeling error controlled by the material
    contrast.

  @section guides-shapeopt-loop The optimization loop

  One iteration of level-set shape optimization follows the sequence below.

  @code{.cpp}
  // 1. Discretize the current domain from phi (remesh / register).
  // 2. Solve the state equation on the current domain.
  // 3. Evaluate the objective; assemble the shape differential dJ.
  // 4. Extend/regularize: theta = H1a(vectorFes).setAlpha(alpha)(dJ).
  // 5. Line search on J along theta (with a CFL cap on the step).
  // 6. Advect phi by the accepted step.
  // 7. Redistance phi (every few iterations).
  @endcode

  Judge a run by the objective history, the directional
  derivative check, volume and its specified penalty or constraint,
  interface quality (no inverted elements), and — after changes to any
  derivative — the finite-difference check of
  @ref guides-shapeopt-derivative "the shape derivative".

